{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-1 cap, before the code (1064d0de). Read at main 57e893c0.\n\nWhere it sits. `SqliteStore` is the only `Store` (scope/src/store.ts:70, :82). The Durable Object builds it over ctx.storage.sql (object.ts:213-216), and the CLI's local application builds one too (cli/src/application-local.ts:57). A birth and a copy of a foreign entry are both `retained_input` rows of kind `entry`, keyed by (kind, digest), so each entry is stored once. A key-only `retained_birth` row (sqlite.ts:125-126, :682-685) marks a birth. Neither table has a size column. All retains happen in `#seal` after `append` (turn.ts:507-509). `#full` (turn.ts:466-473) runs after `#seal` in the same transaction. A bound it returns throws `Full`, rolls back and answers `verdict.full` (turn.ts:420-422), which is already a host stop that writes nothing. The check goes in the branch for a scope without the opt-in, beside `fits` (turn.ts:467): return `scopeBytes` when this entry added retained bytes and the new total is over 1,073,741,824. The five `full` answers attach `name` only for declared capacity (core.ts:616, :775, :871, delivery.ts:313, operations.ts:810). Each will pass `name: \"scopeBytes\"` for every scope.\n\n1. Stored total, not a scan. The opt-in check scans every turn today (`logicalBytes`, sqlite.ts:369-388), and a scan here would read the unbounded rows that the cap bounds. The storage is one new row in the existing `meta` table (sqlite.ts:92): key `retained-entry-bytes`, value { bytes, head: { seq, hash } }. It adds no table, column or schema change. `retain` adds a row's size only when its insert writes the row (`INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING octet_length(bytes)`). Kind-`entry` rows are never deleted (`forget` removes texts only, sqlite.ts:691), so the total only rises. Each turn that writes costs one meta read, the same inserts as today, and one meta upsert.\n\n2. A scope made before the head: a lazy fill, with no migration. If the row is missing at a turn, one scan inside the turn's transaction (`SUM(octet_length(bytes)) FROM retained_input WHERE kind = 'entry'`) writes it before the seal. A new meta key needs no Wrangler migration tag: the tags are class-level only (wrangler.artroom-scope.jsonc:39-42), and no code reads all of meta (archive-pin.ts:72 names its keys). Rollback stays open. Rollback guard: old code retains rows without moving the total, so the row stores the head it counts to. If the stored head differs from the scope head before a seal, the turn rescans. One rule covers both a scope made before the head and a roll forward after a rollback. This is stored state, so as you said, the head lands before the cutover deployment.\n\n3. A scope already past the cap refuses only inputs that would add retained bytes. Acts that name no new foreign fact, outcomes that retain nothing, timed entries and floors still pass. Acts and geneses with new facts answer `scope-full` with `scopeBytes`. Deliveries answer retry with `scope-full` (delivery.ts:313), and the sender's outbox keeps retrying. Refusing every input would stop settlements and timed work as well. Nothing brings a scope back under: a floor deletes only entry, journal_kept and size_proof rows (sqlite.ts:340-348), and nothing deletes a kind-`entry` or `retained_birth` row. The stop is therefore permanent for inputs that add bytes, and results from children stall. The first-fill scan reports the total so that an operator can see a scope that starts over the cap.\n\nTwo questions:\n(a) Texts, rule inputs, definitions, snapshots and `retained_value` rows are also never reclaimed by a floor. Your ruling names births and copies only. Should the total count every retained kind, or births and copies alone?\n(b) Recovery for a scope at the cap, for example pruning rows that an archive pass already holds (the `pass` column, sqlite.ts:84), is a later request, not this head. Confirm.\n\nI build when you answer (a). Without an answer, the head counts births and copies only, as ruled."}